// draft
// drafted
type Pool = { values: Text[] }
// drafted
type Row = { values: Text[] }

partial_product(iterables: Pool[], repeat: Int) -> Row[]
	note "starting from every pool's first value, advance the pools (the iterables listed in order, the whole list taken repeat times) one at a time from first to last, emitting a tuple after each step, so every value of every pool is emitted without the full product"
	# Every output tuple has exactly as many values as there are pools.

	# Each value in an output tuple is one of the values of the pool at that position.

	# The output is empty exactly when some pool is empty.

	# When no pool is empty, the first tuple holds the first value of every pool.

	# Each tuple after the first is the tuple before it with one position set to that pool's next value and every other position kept.

	# The position that advances never moves back to an earlier pool from one tuple to the next.

	# Every value of every pool appears at that pool's position in some output tuple.

	// example wanted: the docstring case, iterables "AB", "C", "DEF" at repeat 1: the single-value middle pool never advances, so the advancing position goes straight from the first pool to the third
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" at repeat 3: decided by the note's expansion into three pools "AB", "AB", "AB", each advanced in turn
	// example wanted: no iterables at repeat 1: there are no pools, none of them is empty, so the first-tuple sentence applies to a tuple with no values
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "C" at repeat 0: repeating the list zero times leaves no pools, the same case as no iterables
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" at repeat -1: a negative repeat leaves no pools, as Python's tuple times a negative number gives the empty tuple
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "", "C" at repeat 1: one empty pool, decided by the empty-output sentence
	// example wanted: iterables "AB", "" at repeat 0: the empty iterable is never repeated into a pool, so no pool is empty
	// example wanted: iterables "AA", "B" at repeat 1: advancing to the next value of the first pool sets the same value again, so two consecutive tuples are equal
	// example wanted: iterables "ABC" at repeat 1: a single pool, so every tuple has one value and the pool advances through all three
